<SupportingGuide path="/lawsuits/roundup/proof/" introduction="A useful Roundup record file connects the product, exposure history, diagnosis, and any earlier legal action. This guide explains how to organize that information without treating a checklist as a decision about eligibility.">
  <section id="proof-purpose">
    <h2>Proof for which process?</h2>
    <p>A lawsuit review and a settlement application are different tasks. The King v. Monsanto settlement FAQ describes its own product, exposure, medical, and prior-claim criteria. Those criteria should not be treated as a universal test for every Roundup lawsuit. Monsanto disputes the cancer allegations. <a href={faq}>Source: official settlement FAQ, questions 2, 8–9 and 17–18</a>.</p>
    <p>Start the file with a short cover note stating its purpose: first legal review, an existing court case, or a particular settlement application. Include the court and case number if one already exists. This keeps records for separate proceedings from being mixed together.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="records-checklist">
    <h2>A practical record checklist</h2>
    <p>The following is an organizational aid, not a court form or an exhaustive list of required proof.</p>
    <ul class="list-disc">
      <li><strong>Product identification:</strong> collect existing receipts, dated photographs, packaging images, purchase histories, or application invoices. Record the exact product name rather than relying only on the word “weedkiller.”</li>
      <li><strong>Exposure timeline:</strong> list locations, approximate years, tasks, frequency, and who may remember the work. Label estimates as estimates and distinguish personal memories from records.</li>
      <li><strong>Work history:</strong> keep employer names, job descriptions, payroll or tax records, and business records together. The settlement FAQ specifically discusses documentary support for occupational status.</li>
      <li><strong>Medical file:</strong> identify the diagnosing provider, diagnosis date, pathology report, and treatment records. Use the diagnosis written in the records rather than guessing a lymphoma subtype.</li>
      <li><strong>Prior legal paperwork:</strong> collect complaints, docket numbers, tolling agreements, release documents, and any settlement correspondence for a lawyer to review.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href={faq}>Source: official settlement FAQ, questions 8–9, 12, 17–19 and 22</a>. Its evidentiary and classification rules apply to that settlement process.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="missing-records">
    <h2>What if receipts or older records are missing?</h2>
    <p>Make a separate list of gaps: what is missing, who might hold it, and which dates remain uncertain. A former employer, retailer account, medical provider, or family photo archive may be a place to look. Preserve the distinction between an original record and a later reconstruction.</p>
    <p>The settlement FAQ distinguishes stronger documentary proof from a personal statement. A missing receipt should therefore prompt a closer look at the actual rules and available evidence; this page cannot determine whether an alternative will be accepted. <a href={faq}>Source: official settlement FAQ, questions 19 and 22</a>.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="review-next">
    <h2>Keep proof and deadlines together</h2>
    <p>Date the record inventory and keep a copy of any request for missing documents. Use the <a href="/lawsuits/roundup/deadline/">Roundup deadline guide</a> to distinguish state filing issues from settlement dates. Do not assume that collecting records or signing up for news resolves either issue.</p>
    <p>This site does not accept claim applications or medical files. The <a href="/lawsuits/roundup/">national Roundup guide</a> provides the broader litigation context, and the official administrator provides the settlement's current forms and instructions.</p>
  </section>
</SupportingGuide>
